; Cosmos pushdown facts as an egglog program.
;
; A prototype of what apache-calcite-cosmos's Metadata/ layer (CosmosFact, CosmosFactTheory,
; CosmosSchemaFacts, CosmosFactRewriter) would look like on an equality-saturation engine. Each
; scenario file includes this prelude, states what a schema compiler would emit for one container,
; asserts one query, and checks what can and cannot be proven.
;
; Four rulesets, so a scenario can run with less than all of them and show the difference:
;
;   horn     - inheritance between contexts, guarded schema rules, query conjuncts as facts.
;              Roughly what the adapter's definite Horn theory does today.
;   lattice  - combining claims about one path: domains intersect, a disequality narrows a
;              domain, an empty domain or interval is a contradiction. Replaces the hand-written
;              CosmosFact.Entails / Excludes tables.
;   split    - case splitting on a finite domain, and lifting what every case proves back to the
;              context that was split. This is the part Horn cannot do.
;   rewrite  - predicate rewrites licensed by the facts, plus boolean simplification. Extraction
;              then picks the cheapest equivalent predicate.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Contexts
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Every fact is held relative to a context. (Schema) is what the container's declaration says on
; its own. (Query) is the schema plus the conjuncts of the query being planned. (Assume c p v) is
; c, plus "path p is present and holds v": one case of a split.
;
; Keeping Schema and Query apart is what stops a conjunct being used to prove itself redundant:
; a conjunct may be deleted only when the *schema* entails it, while it may be lowered when the
; *query* (which still contains the guard) entails what the lowering needs.

(datatype Ctx)
(constructor Schema () Ctx)
(constructor Query () Ctx)
(constructor Assume (Ctx String String) Ctx)

(relation parent (Ctx Ctx))
(parent (Query) (Schema))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Claims about a path: lattices rather than atoms
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Every claim except presence is "if the path holds a value". A missing entry is "unknown", the
; top of the lattice. Merges only ever narrow, which is what makes the rules monotone.

(sort Strs (Set String))

(function dom (Ctx String) Strs :merge (set-intersect old new))   ; value is one of these
(function types (Ctx String) Strs :merge (set-intersect old new)) ; JSON type is one of these
(function lo (Ctx String) i64 :merge (max old new))               ; numeric value >= lo
(function hi (Ctx String) i64 :merge (min old new))               ; numeric value <= hi
(relation present (Ctx String))                                   ; IS_DEFINED holds
(relation form (Ctx String String))                               ; strings use this stored form
(relation ne (Ctx String String))                                 ; value is not this
(relation contradiction (Ctx))                                    ; no document is in this context

; What the schema compiler says may be split on: a path, and the finite set of values it can be.
; A discriminator property is one; so is the hidden index of an undiscriminated anyOf.
(relation discriminator (String String))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; horn: inheritance
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(ruleset horn)

(rule ((parent k c) (= d (dom c p)))   ((set (dom k p) d))   :ruleset horn)
(rule ((parent k c) (= t (types c p))) ((set (types k p) t)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((parent k c) (= l (lo c p)))    ((set (lo k p) l))    :ruleset horn)
(rule ((parent k c) (= h (hi c p)))    ((set (hi k p) h))    :ruleset horn)
(rule ((parent k c) (present c p))     ((present k p))       :ruleset horn)
(rule ((parent k c) (form c p f))      ((form k p f))        :ruleset horn)
(rule ((parent k c) (ne c p v))        ((ne k p v))          :ruleset horn)
(rule ((parent k c) (contradiction c)) ((contradiction k))   :ruleset horn)

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; lattice: combining claims about one path
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(ruleset lattice)

; Domains here hold strings only, so a known domain settles the type.
(rule ((= d (dom c p))) ((set (types c p) (set-of "string"))) :ruleset lattice)

; A stored form describes strings, and says nothing about a null beside them.
(rule ((form c p f)) ((set (types c p) (set-of "string" "null"))) :ruleset lattice)

; A disequality removes its value from a known domain. CosmosFact.Knows asks whether one known
; fact entails the target, so OneOf{a,b} and NotEqualTo(b) never meet there to make EqualTo(a).
(rule ((ne c p v) (= d (dom c p)) (set-contains d v))
      ((set (dom c p) (set-remove d v)))
      :ruleset lattice)

; A path that is present and can hold nothing, or whose bounds cross, has no document.
(rule ((present c p) (= d (dom c p)) (= 0 (set-length d)))   ((contradiction c)) :ruleset lattice)
(rule ((present c p) (= t (types c p)) (= 0 (set-length t))) ((contradiction c)) :ruleset lattice)
(rule ((present c p) (= l (lo c p)) (= h (hi c p)) (> l h))  ((contradiction c)) :ruleset lattice)

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; split: case analysis, and lifting what every case agrees on
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Where a discriminator is present and its domain has more than one value, make one child context
; per remaining value. A child is its parent plus "p = v", so it inherits everything above.
;
; Soundness of a lift rests on one thing: the cases cover the parent. They do because the split
; only happens where p is *present* and every value p can still hold has a case. Domains only
; shrink, so a case created early that later turns out impossible makes the requirement stronger,
; never weaker.

(ruleset split)

(rule ((discriminator p v) (present c p) (= d (dom c p)) (> (set-length d) 1) (set-contains d v))
      ((parent (Assume c p v) c)
       (present (Assume c p v) p)
       (set (dom (Assume c p v) p) (set-of v)))
      :ruleset split)

; A case with no document removes its value from the parent's domain. If every case goes, the
; parent's domain is empty and the lattice rules call the parent a contradiction.
(rule ((= k (Assume c p v)) (contradiction k) (= d (dom c p)) (set-contains d v))
      ((set (dom c p) (set-remove d v)))
      :ruleset split)

; Lift a stored form: the values of p whose case proves it, against the values p can hold.
(function form-cases (Ctx String String String) Strs :merge (set-union old new))
(rule ((= k (Assume c p v)) (form k q f))
      ((set (form-cases c p q f) (set-of v)))
      :ruleset split)
(rule ((= s (form-cases c p q f)) (present c p) (= d (dom c p)) (= 0 (set-length (set-diff d s))))
      ((form c q f))
      :ruleset split)

; Lift presence the same way.
(function present-cases (Ctx String String) Strs :merge (set-union old new))
(rule ((= k (Assume c p v)) (present k q))
      ((set (present-cases c p q) (set-of v)))
      :ruleset split)
(rule ((= s (present-cases c p q)) (present c p) (= d (dom c p)) (= 0 (set-length (set-diff d s))))
      ((present c q))
      :ruleset split)

; Lift a domain as the *union* of the cases' domains, once every case has reported one. This is
; the semantic join that the adapter's syntactic meet of rule heads approximates: EqualTo "x" in
; one branch and EqualTo "y" in the other is OneOf {x, y} here and nothing there.
(function dom-cases (Ctx String String) Strs :merge (set-union old new))
(function dom-reported (Ctx String String) Strs :merge (set-union old new))
(rule ((= k (Assume c p v)) (= dq (dom k q)))
      ((set (dom-cases c p q) dq)
       (set (dom-reported c p q) (set-of v)))
      :ruleset split)
(rule ((= r (dom-reported c p q)) (= u (dom-cases c p q)) (present c p) (= d (dom c p))
       (= 0 (set-length (set-diff d r))))
      ((set (dom c q) u))
      :ruleset split)

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Predicates
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Cost is what the service charges for a predicate's shape, roughly. A comparison with no Cosmos
; form costs the container read whole, which is what the 1000 stands for.

(datatype Pred
  (PTrue :cost 0)
  (PFalse :cost 0)
  (And Pred Pred :cost 1)
  (Or Pred Pred :cost 1)
  (Not Pred :cost 1)
  (StrEq String String :cost 1)    ; c.p = 'v'
  (StrGe String String :cost 1)    ; c.p >= 'v'
  (StrGt String String :cost 1)    ; c.p > 'v'
  (BoolTrue String :cost 1)        ; c.p = true
  (NumGt String i64 :cost 1)       ; c.p > n
  (IsDefined String :cost 1)       ; IS_DEFINED(c.p)
  (IsString String :cost 1)        ; IS_STRING(c.p)
  (UuidEq String String :cost 1000)  ; CAST(JSON_VALUE(doc, '$.p') AS UUID) = UUID'v', in process
  (InstantGt String String :cost 1000)) ; CAST(... AS TIMESTAMP) > TIMESTAMP'v', in process

; The query's WHERE clause, and each of its top-level conjuncts.
(relation where (Pred))
(relation conjunct (Pred))
(rule ((where e)) ((conjunct e)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((conjunct (And a b))) ((conjunct a) (conjunct b)) :ruleset horn)

; Which values of a path a predicate lets through, as two analyses over the e-graph:
;   (inset e p)  = S  means e is exactly "p is present and one of S"  (an IN list, however spelt)
;   (admits e p) = S  means e implies "p is present and one of S"     (a conjunct or every disjunct says so)
; Both merge by intersection: two equivalent spellings both hold, so their sets do too. This is
; what makes (kind = 'a' AND x) OR (kind = 'b' AND y) establish kind IN (a, b) without a rewrite
; to factor it first.
(function inset (Pred String) Strs :merge (set-intersect old new))
(function admits (Pred String) Strs :merge (set-intersect old new))
(rule ((= e (StrEq p v))) ((set (inset e p) (set-of v))) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((= e (Or a b)) (= sa (inset a p)) (= sb (inset b p))) ((set (inset e p) (set-union sa sb))) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((= s (inset e p))) ((set (admits e p) s)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((= e (And a b)) (= s (admits a p))) ((set (admits e p) s)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((= e (And a b)) (= s (admits b p))) ((set (admits e p) s)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((= e (Or a b)) (= sa (admits a p)) (= sb (admits b p))) ((set (admits e p) (set-union sa sb))) :ruleset horn)

; What a conjunct establishes about the documents that pass it. A SQL comparison is unknown over
; a missing path, and an unknown conjunct drops the row, so every comparison proves presence.
(rule ((conjunct e) (= s (admits e p)))
      ((present (Query) p) (set (dom (Query) p) s))
      :ruleset horn)
(rule ((conjunct (Not (StrEq p v))))
      ((present (Query) p) (ne (Query) p v))
      :ruleset horn)
(rule ((conjunct (UuidEq p v))) ((present (Query) p)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((conjunct (StrGe p v))) ((present (Query) p)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((conjunct (InstantGt p v))) ((present (Query) p)) :ruleset horn)
(rule ((conjunct (NumGt p n)))
      ((present (Query) p) (set (lo (Query) p) (+ n 1)) (set (types (Query) p) (set-of "number")))
      :ruleset horn)

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; rewrite: what the facts license
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(ruleset rewrite)

; CosmosFactRewriter.TryLowerUuid: a canonical-lowercase stored form makes the string equality
; answer exactly what the UUID equality answers. Judged in (Query), because the guard that proved
; the form is a conjunct of this same predicate and stays in it.
(rule ((= e (UuidEq p v)) (form (Query) p "uuid-lower"))
      ((union e (StrEq p v)))
      :ruleset rewrite)

; CosmosFactRewriter.TryLowerInstant: one fixed-width UTC spelling sorts as the instants do, so a
; range lowers too. (The literal is assumed already rendered in that spelling.)
(rule ((= e (InstantGt p v)) (form (Query) p "instant-ms"))
      ((union e (StrGt p v)))
      :ruleset rewrite)

; Soundness of the two lowerings rests on an invariant the rules below keep: a fact in (Query) is
; implied by the conjuncts that proved it, and no rewrite judged on (Query) deletes a conjunct.
; Only (Schema) facts delete. So the rewritten predicate still implies every (Query) fact the
; rewrites relied on -- the adapter's "the guard stays in the predicate", stated once.

; A predicate the declaration contradicts keeps nothing (AContradictedPredicateIsReducedToTheConstant).
(rule ((where e) (contradiction (Query))) ((union e (PFalse))) :ruleset rewrite)

; A conjunct the *schema* guarantees is not asked (ADeclaredTautologyIsNotAsked) -- an equality
; against a required const, or an IN list covering a required enum.
(rule ((= s (inset e p)) (present (Schema) p) (= d (dom (Schema) p)) (= 0 (set-length (set-diff d s))))
      ((union e (PTrue)))
      :ruleset rewrite)

; The weakened comparison is IS_DEFINED(p) AND (NOT IS_STRING(p) OR cmp). Where the path holds
; only strings (or nulls, which cmp rejects anyway), NOT IS_STRING admits nothing a present path
; holds, so it is NOT IS_DEFINED -- and the definedness test that existed for its sake goes with it.
(rule ((= e (Not (IsString p))) (= t (types (Query) p))
       (= 0 (set-length (set-diff t (set-of "string" "null")))))
      ((union e (Not (IsDefined p))))
      :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (And (IsDefined p) (Or (Not (IsDefined p)) x)) (And (IsDefined p) x) :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (And (IsDefined p) (StrGe p v)) (StrGe p v) :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (And (IsDefined p) (StrEq p v)) (StrEq p v) :ruleset rewrite)
(rule ((= e (IsDefined p)) (present (Schema) p)) ((union e (PTrue))) :ruleset rewrite)

; Boolean simplification.
(rewrite (And a b) (And b a) :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (Or a b) (Or b a) :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (And (PTrue) a) a :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (And (PFalse) a) (PFalse) :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (Or (PFalse) a) a :ruleset rewrite)
(rewrite (Or (PTrue) a) (PTrue) :ruleset rewrite)
